---
import SquarespaceFooter from '../components/SquarespaceFooter.astro';
import SquarespaceHeader from '../components/SquarespaceHeader.astro';
import type { ShellConfig } from '../config/shell';
import { createShellConfig } from '../config/shell';
import { createLessonImages } from '../config/lessonImages';
import { actPunctuationLessonNode } from '../config/schema';
import SquarespaceShellLayout from '../layouts/SquarespaceShellLayout.astro';

const defaultConfig = createShellConfig({
  siteTitle: 'ACT Punctuation Rules: Commas, Colons & Dashes',
  description: 'Every punctuation rule tested on the ACT — complete sentences, run-ons, colons, commas and dashes — with the steps for each question type.',
  schema: [actPunctuationLessonNode],
});

const dateModified = actPunctuationLessonNode.dateModified as string;
const formatDate = (iso: string) =>
  new Date(`${iso}T00:00:00`).toLocaleDateString('en-US', { month: 'long', day: 'numeric', year: 'numeric' });
const { config = defaultConfig }: { config?: ShellConfig } = Astro.props;

const img = await createLessonImages(
  import.meta.glob<{ default: ImageMetadata }>('../assets/lessons/act-punctuation/*.png', { eager: true }),
);

const navItems = [
  { href: '#prerequisites', label: 'Prerequisites' },
  { href: '#complete-complete', label: '"Complete | Complete"' },
  { href: '#run-ons', label: 'Run-ons' },
  { href: '#colons', label: 'Colons' },
  { href: '#commas', label: 'Commas' },
  { href: '#dashes', label: 'Dashes' },
  { href: '#title-occupation-name', label: 'Title/Occupation + Name' },
];
---
<SquarespaceShellLayout config={config}>
  <div id="siteWrapper" class="clearfix site-wrapper">
    <SquarespaceHeader config={config} />

    <main id="page" class="ht-main" tabindex="-1">

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 1 — HERO
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-pn-hero">
        <div class="ht-pn-hero__inner">
          <p class="ht-eyebrow ht-eyebrow--crimson">ACT ENGLISH</p>
          <h1 class="ht-pn-hero__h1">Mastering ACT Punctuation Rules</h1>
          <p class="ht-pn-hero__lead">
            Much of the English section of the ACT tests your knowledge of
            punctuation, which is, in many ways, a test of sentence structure.
            In real-life English, punctuation rules are expansive, covering all
            kinds of nuanced, specific rules. Fortunately for us, the ACT’s
            punctuation questions are limited and repetitive. If you learn
            these question types, how to spot them, and how to process them,
            it’s very simple. This guide will explain exactly how to do so.
          </p>
          <p class="ht-pn-hero__byline">
            By <a href="/about">Majid Hasan</a>, ACT &amp; SAT tutor ·
            Updated <time datetime={dateModified}>{formatDate(dateModified)}</time>
          </p>
          <p class="ht-pn-hero__crosslink">
            Studying for the SAT? See the <a href="/sat-punctuation-guide">SAT punctuation guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ======================================================
           LEFT NAV + CONTENT LAYOUT
           ====================================================== -->
      <div class="ht-pn-layout">
        <aside class="ht-pn-nav" aria-label="Lesson sections">
          <nav class="ht-pn-nav__inner">
            <p class="ht-pn-nav__label">On this page</p>
            <ul class="ht-pn-nav__list">
              {navItems.map((item) => (
                <li>
                  <a href={item.href} class="ht-pn-nav__link" data-nav-link>{item.label}</a>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </nav>
        </aside>

        <div class="ht-pn-content">

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 2 — PREREQUISITES
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-pn-section" id="prerequisites">
        <div class="ht-pn-section__inner">
          <div class="ht-section-hdr">
            <p class="ht-eyebrow ht-eyebrow--gold">Prerequisite</p>
            <h2 class="ht-section-hdr__h2">What counts as a complete sentence?</h2>
            <p class="ht-section-hdr__lead">
              To use these processes, you need to be able to discern between a
              complete and incomplete sentence and independent and dependent
              clauses. If you can read a phrase and determine if it’s a complete
              sentence, everything else will fall into place. So what is a
              complete sentence, or rather, how should we think about them on
              the ACT? Think of them like this: a complete sentence is exactly
              one Independent clause + Literally any number of dependent
              clauses, including zero dependent clauses.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="ht-pn-h3">Independent clauses</h3>
          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            An independent clause has a subject and a verb. You can usually
            just tell: if it sounds like it could stand on its own, or like a
            complete thought, it's an independent clause and therefore a
            complete sentence by itself. A few examples:
          </p>

          <ul class="ht-styled-list">
            <li>The committee reached a compromise after weeks of unproductive debate.</li>
            <li>The engineer revised the design so that it could withstand greater pressure.</li>
            <li>The student who had submitted her essay late asked for additional feedback.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="ht-pn-h3">Dependent clauses</h3>
          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Dependent clauses can't stand on their own. You don't need to
            memorize grammar terminology; just learn to recognize these four
            common patterns.
          </p>

          <div class="ht-pn-card-grid">
            <div class="ht-pn-card">
              <span class="ht-pn-card__num" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
              <h4 class="ht-pn-card__h4">Phrases that set time or place</h4>
              <p class="ht-pn-card__body">Prepositional phrases are phrases that set time and place by starting with words like "As," "While," "In," "At," "Near," "Above," "Below," and similar words.</p>
              <ul class="ht-pn-card__ex">
                <li>In the 1950s</li>
                <li>While the board members debated the merits of the proposal</li>
                <li>Over twenty years ago</li>
                <li>Above the building</li>
                <li>Formed in 1967 to foster political and economic stability within the Asia-Pacific region</li>
                <li>When the manuscript was returned to the author with extensive notes and corrections</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="ht-pn-card">
              <span class="ht-pn-card__num" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
              <h4 class="ht-pn-card__h4">Conditional phrases</h4>
              <p class="ht-pn-card__body">Phrases that start with words like "Because," "If," "Although," "Since."</p>
              <ul class="ht-pn-card__ex">
                <li>Since the creation of the oversight committee</li>
                <li>If the leverage ratio reaches a certain point</li>
                <li>Because these organisms are so tiny</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="ht-pn-card">
              <span class="ht-pn-card__num" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
              <h4 class="ht-pn-card__h4">Phrases that start with an "ing" word.</h4>
              <p class="ht-pn-card__body">A very common pattern that's easy to spot once you're looking for it.</p>
              <ul class="ht-pn-card__ex">
                <li>…, starting with the lowest number.</li>
                <li>…, solidifying her position within the organization.</li>
                <li>…, forming a layer of sedimentary bedrock.</li>
                <li>…, ensuring that proteins retain their original configurations.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="ht-pn-card">
              <span class="ht-pn-card__num" aria-hidden="true">4</span>
              <h4 class="ht-pn-card__h4">Interjections</h4>
              <p class="ht-pn-card__body">
                A dependent clause tucked inside another clause. If you can
                remove the phrase and the sentence still flows, it's an
                interjection. They're usually found inside independent
                clauses, but on harder questions can appear inside a
                dependent clause too. They're explicitly tested with commas
                and dashes, and recognizing — then mentally removing — them
                makes many questions far easier.
              </p>
              <ul class="ht-pn-card__ex">
                <li>The company, <em>though battered by years of declining profits</em>, announced an ambitious new initiative.</li>
                <li>In The Book of Salt, <strong>for example</strong>, the exiled character of Bình connects to his native Saigon through the food he prepares</li>
                <li>The artwork, <em>though controversial at its unveiling</em>, has since become widely celebrated.</li>
                <li>The invention, <em>which transformed communication across continents</em>, changed history.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="ht-pn-h3">Common sentence-structure patterns</h3>
          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            These are the shapes you'll see over and over on the ACT. Getting
            fast at spotting them makes every boundary question easier.
          </p>

          <div class="ht-pn-pattern-list">
            <div class="ht-pn-pattern">
              <p class="ht-pn-pattern__label">Independent clause only</p>
              <ul class="ht-styled-list">
                <li>The engineer revised the design so that it could withstand greater pressure.</li>
                <li>Beatrix Potter is perhaps best known for writing and illustrating children's books such as The Tale of Peter Rabbit (1902).</li>
                <li>The orchestra performed a piece that blended classical structure with modern dissonance.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="ht-pn-pattern">
              <p class="ht-pn-pattern__label">Dependent, Independent</p>
              <ul class="ht-styled-list">
                <li>In 1933, the Twentieth Amendment to the US Constitution was ratified.</li>
                <li>In her poetry collection Thomas and Beulah, Rita Dove interweaves the titular characters' personal stories with broader historical narratives.</li>
                <li>In a study of the cognitive abilities of white-faced capuchin monkeys (Cebus imitator), researchers neglected to control for the physical difficulty of the tasks they used to evaluate the monkeys.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="ht-pn-pattern">
              <p class="ht-pn-pattern__label">Independent, Dependent</p>
              <ul class="ht-styled-list">
                <li>The intense pressure found in the deep ocean can affect the structure of proteins in fish's cells, distorting the proteins' shape.</li>
                <li>The cognitive abilities of monkeys given problems requiring little dexterity were judged by the same criteria as were those of monkeys given physically demanding problems, such as unscrewing a bottle and inserting a straw.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="ht-pn-pattern">
              <p class="ht-pn-pattern__label">Dependent, Independent, Dependent</p>
              <ul class="ht-styled-list">
                <li>In the 1950s, a man named Joseph McVicker was struggling to keep his business afloat when his sister-in-law Kay Zufall advised him to repurpose the company's product — a nontoxic, clay-like substance for removing soot from wallpaper — as a modeling putty for kids.</li>
                <li>Compiled in the late 1500s largely through the efforts of Indigenous scribes, Cantares Mexicanos is the most important collection of poetry in Classical Nahuatl, the principal language of the Aztec Empire.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 3 — "COMPLETE | COMPLETE"
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-pn-section ht-pn-section--tint" id="complete-complete">
        <div class="ht-pn-section__inner">
          <div class="ht-section-hdr">
            <p class="ht-eyebrow ht-eyebrow--gold">The core rule</p>
            <h2 class="ht-section-hdr__h2">"Complete|Complete"</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            These three types of punctuation marks require complete
            sentences before them AND after them: periods, semicolons, and
            comma+FANBOYS ("<span class="ht-pn-mono">,and</span>"
            "<span class="ht-pn-mono">,but</span>"). Not just a comma, and
            not just a FANBOYS, but a comma right before a FANBOYS together
            forms one punctuation mark, equal to a period and semicolon.
          </p>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            So, whenever you're testing one of these three, read up to the
            point and check if it's a complete sentence. If it's not, then
            it's incorrect. Then read from that point to the end of the
            sentence and do the same check. Let's work through some
            examples.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img {...img('P1')} alt="Diagram: 'complete?' on both sides of a sentence boundary, arrows meeting at a dashed divider." loading="lazy" />
            <img {...img('P2')} alt="Same diagram with boxes below showing a semicolon, a period, and a comma-plus-'and' as the possible boundary marks." loading="lazy" />
            <img {...img('P3')} alt="Worked example: 'I took the ACT, and crushed it.' marked wrong, and 'I took the ACT, and I crushed it.' marked correct." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Here we see a semi-colon in answer choice C. Read from the
            beginning of the sentence up to the semi-colon. We see that it’s
            the only answer choice with a complete sentence before AND after
            the semicolon.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img {...img('P4')} alt="ACT-style question 19 about spectators talking to each other instead of on their phones, testing punctuation between 'phones' and 'they talked'." loading="lazy" />
            <div class="ht-pn-imgstack">
              <img {...img('P6')} alt="Reasoning notes: 'Let's test answer choice A) — read up to the semicolon and see if it's complete — then read from the semicolon to the period and see if it's complete.'" loading="lazy" />
              <img {...img('P6_2')} alt="Answer choice A: 'Spectators didn't talk on their phones' and 'they talked to each other' are each marked complete on either side of the semicolon." loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Here we see a comma+FANBOYS
            ("<span class="ht-pn-mono">,but</span>") in answer choice D. Read
            from the beginning of the sentence up to the
            "<span class="ht-pn-mono">,but</span>". We see right away that
            it's not a complete sentence so D is out, since a comma+FANBOYS
            requires a complete sentence before and after.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img {...img('P7')} alt="ACT-style question 39 about White finding the work revelatory, testing punctuation between 'revelatory' and 'he's learning.'" loading="lazy" />
            <img {...img('P7_2')} alt="Answer choice C circled, with the clauses on both sides of the semicolon each marked complete." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Here's an example when a comma+FANBOYS is
            correct, answer choice B
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <div class="ht-pn-imgstack">
              <img {...img('P11')} alt="ACT-style question 21 about the artist Torres's Sun Light series, testing punctuation around 'art' with a comma-plus-'and' answer choice." loading="lazy" />
              <img {...img('P11_2')} alt="Answer choice B, with the clauses on both sides of the comma-plus-'and' each marked complete." loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Here we see an example of a question where the Complete Complete
            punctuation mark, here a semicolon, does not work. Reading up to
            the semicolon in choice B, we see it’s complete. However, from
            there to the period is not, so B is out.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <div class="ht-pn-imgstack">
              <img {...img('P12')} alt="ACT-style question 37 about Jones refusing to sell his land, testing punctuation between 'land' and 'because'." loading="lazy" />
              <img {...img('P12_2')} alt="Answer choice B crossed out: the clause before the semicolon is complete, but 'because he wanted the area to be conserved' is not." loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 4 — RUN-ONS
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-pn-section" id="run-ons">
        <div class="ht-pn-section__inner">
          <div class="ht-section-hdr">
            <p class="ht-eyebrow ht-eyebrow--gold">What to avoid</p>
            <h2 class="ht-section-hdr__h2">Run-ons</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            <strong>What they are.</strong> Run-ons are where you have two
            complete sentences connected by only a comma, only a FANBOYS, or
            by nothing.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img {...img('P13')} alt="Diagram labeled 'Run-Ons' showing two complete sentences joined incorrectly by only a comma, only a FANBOYS word, or nothing, with a red X." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            On the ACT, they're usually tested as incorrect answers when you
            see that there are answers with periods, semicolons,
            comma+FANBOYS, or colons (which we'll talk about soon). However,
            we'll see some where they're testing on their own.
          </p>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Let's revisit this problem. We already saw that there were two
            complete sentences. One up to the word "art" and another from
            "in 2016" to the period. A separates them with nothing, C with
            only a FANBOYS ("and"), and D with only a comma.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img {...img('P11')} alt="ACT-style question 21 about the artist Torres's Sun Light series, testing punctuation around 'art' with a comma-plus-'and' answer choice." loading="lazy" />
            <img {...img('P11_3')} alt="Answer choice A: 'Interconnectedness eventually…Torres's art' and 'in 2016, with…series, he paid homage… inspired him' are each complete, with nothing between them — a run-on." loading="lazy" />
            <div class="ht-pn-imgstack">
              <img {...img('P11_4')} alt="Answer choice C: the same two complete clauses joined by only 'and' — a run-on." loading="lazy" />
              <img {...img('P11_5')} alt="Answer choice D: the same two complete clauses joined by only a comma — a comma splice." loading="lazy" />
              <img {...img('P11_6')} alt="Question 21 with A, C, and D crossed out as run-ons — A has nothing, C only a FANBOYS, D only a comma — leaving B." loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Revisiting another problem. We already know that B is an
            incorrect use of a semicolon, but notice that C and D create Run
            Ons.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <div class="ht-pn-imgstack">
              <img {...img('P12')} alt="ACT-style question 37 about Jones refusing to sell his land, testing punctuation between 'land' and 'because'." loading="lazy" />
              <img {...img('P12_3')} alt="Answer choices B, C, and D crossed out: B uses a semicolon without two complete clauses, C joins two complete clauses with only a comma, and D joins them with nothing — both run-ons." loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Here we see an example of a question where there are no ‘Complete
            Complete’ punctuation options nor a colon. You can identify that
            B, C, and D create Run Ons, while A creates a common sentence
            structure of Independent – Dependent as we saw at the beginning of
            this guide.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <div class="ht-pn-imgstack">
              <img {...img('P20')} alt="ACT-style question 23 about a robot's flippers reversing direction in shallow water, testing what follows the comma after 'water'." loading="lazy" />
              <img {...img('P20_2')} alt="Diagram: the clause ending in 'water' is complete, so with a comma after it, choices B, C, and D would put a complete clause after the comma — all eliminated." loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            And another nearly identical problem.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <div class="ht-pn-imgstack">
              <img {...img('P22')} alt="ACT-style question 50 about a researcher who later expanded her study of sharks, testing what follows the comma after 'study'." loading="lazy" />
              <img {...img('P22_2')} alt="Diagram: 'She later expanded the study' is complete, so any choice that puts another complete clause after the comma is a run-on — two completes with only a comma." loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 5 — COLONS
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-pn-section ht-pn-section--tint" id="colons">
        <div class="ht-pn-section__inner">
          <div class="ht-section-hdr">
            <p class="ht-eyebrow ht-eyebrow--gold">A different pattern</p>
            <h2 class="ht-section-hdr__h2">Colons</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            <strong>The rule for the ACT.</strong> Colons can be used in
            many different ways, but on the ACT, it goes like this: you
            MUST have a complete up to the colon, but what comes after just
            has to be an example or explanation of what came before it. It
            can be complete or incomplete. So notice it can have two
            completes but doesn't have to. Just make sure they're two
            different statements before and after.
          </p>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Let's test answer choice A. From "The display" to "mission" is
            complete, so we're good so far. Then what follows the colon to
            the end of the sentence is an explanation of the first phrase.
            A it is.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img {...img('P25')} alt="ACT-style question 38 about Clarke's display being emblematic of his mission, testing whether a colon can follow 'mission.'" loading="lazy" />
            <img {...img('P26')} alt="Answer choice A: 'The display … mission' is complete, and 'making green … accessible' is an example or explanation, so the colon works." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Running the same test, we see that H is correct.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <div class="ht-pn-imgstack">
              <img {...img('P28')} alt="ACT-style question 34 about the extremophile bacterium C. metallidurans, with the colon answer choice highlighted." loading="lazy" />
              <img {...img('P28_2')} alt="Colon test: 'C. metallidurans is an extremophile' is complete, and 'a bacterium … conditions' is an example or explanation, so the colon works." loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            An example where the colon test does not work.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <div class="ht-pn-imgstack">
              <img {...img('P28_3')} alt="ACT-style question 53 about shark intelligence, with the colon after 'springboard' highlighted." loading="lazy" />
              <img {...img('P28_4')} alt="Colon test: 'Using these … springboard' is not a complete sentence, so the colon doesn't work." loading="lazy" />
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 6 — COMMAS
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-pn-section" id="commas">
        <div class="ht-pn-section__inner">
          <div class="ht-section-hdr">
            <p class="ht-eyebrow ht-eyebrow--gold">The two comma rules</p>
            <h2 class="ht-section-hdr__h2">Commas</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Commas are mostly tested in two different ways: to bookend
            interjections and to separate independent and dependent clauses.
            Interjections have to be surrounded by two commas, or as we'll
            see, with dashes. However, it's much more common to see this
            done with commas.
          </p>
          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            The other test of commas follows this rule: on the ACT we
            ALWAYS separate independent and dependent clauses.
          </p>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Check out this question. If we plug in answer choice J, we see
            that we add a comma after “centuries”. The sentence then reads
            “For instance, Baniwa women have farmed distinctly flavorful
            chili peppers for centuries, <strong>using indigenous agricultural
            techniques</strong>, to create a seasoning called pimenta
            jiquitaia.” When we take out the bolded text now surrounded by
            commas, the sentence flows: <em>Baniwa women have farmed
            distinctly flavorful chili peppers for centuries to create a
            seasoning called pimenta jiquitaia.</em>
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img {...img('P29')} alt="ACT-style question 8 about Baniwa women farming chili peppers, with answer choice J ('centuries, using') circled." loading="lazy" />
            <img {...img('P29_2')} alt="Testing whether 'using … techniques' between the commas is removable." loading="lazy" />
            <img {...img('P29_3')} alt="With the phrase removed, 'peppers for centuries to create a seasoning called…' still reads correctly, so it is removable." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Another quick example. B) Since we can remove the phrase "a
            medieval author and dramatist" choice B is correct. Notice that
            A) has a dash after “dramatist”. As we’ll see, that would have
            worked if there were also a dash after “author”.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img {...img('P45')} alt="ACT-style question 11 about the medieval playwright Hrosvitha, with answer choice B ('author and dramatist,') circled." loading="lazy" />
            <img {...img('P45_2')} alt="Answer choice B: 'a medieval author and dramatist' is removable, so it can sit between two commas." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Here's two more examples that work the exact same way.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img {...img('P53')} alt="ACT-style question 47 about Synsepalum dulcificum, with answer choice A circled; the phrase 'called miracle fruit' is marked removable." loading="lazy" />
            <img {...img('P54')} alt="ACT-style question 21 about undersea equipment, with answer choice B ('equipment, however,') circled; 'however' is marked removable." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Let's check out an example of the second way the ACT tests comma
            usage: separating Independent and Dependent clauses. Notice that
            with A) and B) we do not have a required complete up to the colon
            or up to the semicolon. We can see the common
            dependent-independent structure of the sentence, which need to
            be separated. Thus C) is correct.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img {...img('P40')} alt="ACT-style question 53 about shark intelligence, with A and B crossed out and answer choice C ('springboard,') circled." loading="lazy" />
            <img {...img('P55')} alt="'Using these … springboard' is marked not complete, ruling out the colon and semicolon choices." loading="lazy" />
            <img {...img('P56')} alt="Answer choice C: 'Using these … springboard' marked dependent and 'Clark began … animal' marked independent, with the comma between them circled." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            If we read the sentence, we can see that structure again goes
            Dependent-Independent. “Even more fortunately” is dependent and
            “he had already created…” is independent. Thus, we need a comma
            between the two, and D) is correct.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img {...img('P52')} alt="ACT-style question 43 with answer choice D ('Even more fortunately,') circled; 'Even more fortunately' is marked dependent and 'he had already created' independent, with a comma between them." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 7 — DASHES
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-pn-section ht-pn-section--tint" id="dashes">
        <div class="ht-pn-section__inner">
          <div class="ht-section-hdr">
            <p class="ht-eyebrow ht-eyebrow--gold">Rarer, but predictable</p>
            <h2 class="ht-section-hdr__h2">Dashes</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Dashes are tested the exact same ways as the two comma rules we
            just went over, though they are far less common on the ACT. A
            sentence can have only 0, 1, or 2 in a sentence. If you have
            one, it MUST be to separate Independent and Dependent clause. If
            you have two, they MUST be to bookend interjections. They rarely
            ask about Ind. and Dep clauses.
          </p>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Here's an example. Notice there's already a dash in the
            sentence? A pretty big indicator we should test choice B. Read
            the sentence without the phrase "all neighborhood residents and
            most of whom are under the age of thirty" and we see it flows.
            Choice B it is.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img {...img('P44')} alt="ACT-style question 37 about flower-shop employees, with a dash already before 'all neighborhood residents' and answer choice B ('thirty—') circled; the phrase between the dashes is marked removable." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Another example that works the same way.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img {...img('P32')} alt="ACT-style question 27 about Torres's portraits, with A crossed out and answer choice B ('paint—mostly whites, grays, and blacks—') circled." loading="lazy" />
            <img {...img('P68')} alt="Dash test: 'whites, grays, and blacks' between dashes is not removable, but 'mostly whites, grays, and blacks' between dashes is." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 8A — TITLE/OCCUPATION + NAME
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-pn-section" id="title-occupation-name">
        <div class="ht-pn-section__inner">
          <div class="ht-section-hdr">
            <p class="ht-eyebrow ht-eyebrow--gold">Looks like a comma question — isn't</p>
            <h2 class="ht-section-hdr__h2">Title/Occupation + Name</h2>
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            There are their own type of question. Though it looks like it's
            testing comma usage, you should handle it differently. The name
            of the question-type tells you exactly what it is: when you see
            a title/occupation right before a name. "ACT tutor Majid Hasan",
            "theoretical physicist Robert J. Oppenheimer", "cartoon
            character Krusty the Clown", etc.
          </p>
          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            The vast majority of the time, no commas before or after the
            name.
          </p>
          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Check out two examples.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img {...img('P71')} alt="ACT-style question 38 about “farm team” member Dionta White, with answer choice A (no commas around the name) circled and the commas in B, C, and D marked wrong." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img {...img('P72')} alt="ACT-style question 66 about festival organizer Dottie Thompson and hula master “Uncle” George Na‘ope, with answer choice J (no commas) circled and the commas in the original text, G, and H marked wrong." loading="lazy" />
          </div>

          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            <strong>The exception:</strong> every once in a while, you'll
            need a comma after the name (never before) if there's an
            interjection right after the name (or maybe a dependant clause).
            "The book was written by legendary journalist Bob Woodward, who
            won a Pulitzer prize the following year." Here we'd need a comma
            after the name to separate the independent and dependent
            clauses.
          </p>
          <p class="ht-pn-body">
            Here's an example of an interjection after the name. See
            the interjection that starts right after "Ien Chi"? So no comma
            before the name, but we need one right after.
          </p>
          <div class="ht-pn-imgrow">
            <img {...img('P60')} alt="ACT-style question showing an interjection right after the name 'Ien Chi,' requiring a comma after the name but not before it." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- ======================================================
           SECTION 9 — CTA STRIP
           ====================================================== -->
      <section class="ht-cta-strip">
        <h2 class="ht-cta-strip__h2">Want help putting this into practice?</h2>
        <p class="ht-cta-strip__body">
          These patterns click fastest with real ACT questions and someone
          to check your reasoning. I'm happy to walk through a few together.
        </p>
        <div class="ht-cta-strip__btns">
